Privacy Policy for SiteGuard

Effective Date: September 23, 2026

SiteGuard is a browser security extension designed to help users assess the security characteristics of websites they visit. SiteGuard uses a local-first approach and performs security analysis within the user's browser whenever possible.

This Privacy Policy explains what information SiteGuard processes, how that information is used, where it is stored, and what choices users have.

1. Information We Process

SiteGuard is designed to minimize the collection and transmission of user information.

Information processed locally

During a security scan, SiteGuard may process security-relevant information associated with the website being assessed. This may include:

Website URL and URL characteristics
HTTPS and TLS-related indicators
HTTP security response headers
Content-Security-Policy information
Strict-Transport-Security information
Cookie security attributes such as Secure, HttpOnly, and SameSite
Mixed-content indicators
Redirect information
Other technical indicators required to calculate the website security assessment

This information is processed to identify observable security indicators and generate the SiteGuard risk score and findings.

Where possible, this analysis is performed locally within the browser.

2. Information Stored Locally

SiteGuard may store the following information locally in the user's browser:

User scanning preferences
Allow and block lists configured by the user
Security findings
Website risk scores
Local scan history

SiteGuard uses Chrome's extension storage facilities for this information.

The current configuration provides an option to retain local scan history for a limited period. Users can also delete their locally stored scan history through the SiteGuard settings.

SiteGuard does not intentionally store:

Passwords
Authentication credentials
Payment card information
Personal communications
GPS coordinates
Cookie values
Complete webpage HTML
Keystrokes
Mouse movements
Browsing history unrelated to the security scans
3. Optional Security Analysis Backend

SiteGuard can optionally be configured to connect to a Security Analysis API.

The backend is not required for the basic local security scanning functionality.

When the optional backend is not configured, SiteGuard is designed to keep the security analysis local to the user's device.

If a user voluntarily configures an external backend, certain security-analysis information may be transmitted to that backend for the purpose of obtaining additional security intelligence or AI-based classification results.

The optional backend may be used to provide additional:

Threat intelligence findings
Security classification results
AI-assisted security analysis

Users can leave the backend configuration blank to keep SiteGuard in its local-only configuration.

Users should review the privacy policy of any third-party backend or security-analysis service they choose to configure.

4. Purpose of Data Processing

Information processed by SiteGuard is used only to support its core purpose of website security assessment.

This includes:

Detecting observable website security issues
Generating a security risk score
Presenting security findings and supporting evidence
Maintaining user-configured scanning preferences
Maintaining locally stored scan history
Enforcing user-configured allow and block lists
Providing optional additional security analysis when a backend is configured

SiteGuard does not use the information it processes for advertising, behavioral profiling, credit assessment, lending decisions, or unrelated commercial purposes.

5. Data Sharing

SiteGuard does not sell user data.

SiteGuard does not intentionally transfer user information to third parties for advertising or unrelated purposes.

When the optional Security Analysis API is configured by the user, information required to provide the requested additional security analysis may be transmitted to the configured backend.

No optional backend is required for the basic local scanning functionality.

6. Browsing Activity

SiteGuard analyzes websites as part of its core security-scanning function.

The extension may automatically perform a security scan when configured to scan pages as the user visits them.

SiteGuard does not intentionally create or maintain a general-purpose browsing history for advertising, profiling, or tracking purposes.

Any scan history maintained by the extension is associated with SiteGuard's security-analysis functionality and is stored locally in the user's browser.

7. Cookies

SiteGuard may inspect security-related cookie attributes when performing a website security assessment.

SiteGuard does not intentionally collect or store the actual values of users' cookies.

Cookie-related analysis is performed to identify security attributes relevant to the SiteGuard security assessment.

8. Website Content

SiteGuard may inspect technical information available from webpages and network responses when necessary to perform its security assessment.

The extension is designed to focus on security-relevant technical indicators rather than collecting the contents of webpages for unrelated purposes.

SiteGuard does not intentionally store complete webpage HTML as part of its local scan history.

9. Permissions

SiteGuard requests browser permissions that are necessary for its website security analysis functionality.

These permissions may allow SiteGuard to:

Inspect security-relevant information from webpages
Observe response headers required for security analysis
Detect redirect chains
Store extension settings and local scan results
Enforce user-configured website block rules
Perform scheduled updates when the optional backend is configured

The permissions are used to support the extension's core purpose of website security assessment.

10. Data Retention

Information stored by SiteGuard locally remains on the user's device unless the user deletes it or removes the extension.

Where scan-history retention is enabled, users can configure or manage the available retention settings through SiteGuard.

Users can also use the SiteGuard settings to delete locally stored scan history.

Information sent to an optional external backend is subject to the retention practices and privacy policy of that backend.

11. User Control

Users can control several aspects of SiteGuard through the extension's settings, including:

Scanning mode
Scan sensitivity
Allow lists
Block lists
Local scan-history settings
Optional backend configuration

Users can choose not to configure the optional backend and continue using the local security-scanning functionality.

12. Security

SiteGuard is designed with a local-first architecture to reduce unnecessary transmission of security-analysis information.

However, no software or method of electronic storage can guarantee absolute security. Users should exercise appropriate caution when configuring external services or providing information to websites.

13. Children's Privacy

SiteGuard is not specifically directed toward children and does not intentionally collect personal information from children.

14. Changes to This Privacy Policy

This Privacy Policy may be updated when SiteGuard's functionality, data practices, or applicable requirements change.

The updated version will be made available at the same privacy-policy URL.

The effective date at the beginning of this policy will indicate when the policy was most recently updated.

